WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.840
A DAST finding is a potential issue identified from runtime behavior that requires review, context, and disposition.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:19.220
Useful findings include the affected URL or endpoint, request and response evidence, relevant headers or cookies, timing and environment details, severity, confidence, and safe reproduction context.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.040
Evidence helps developers and owners understand what happened without exposing unnecessary sensitive data.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:30.540
Triage asks whether the finding is real, relevant, reachable, and important.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.920
False positives are reports that do not apply as described after review.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.180
False negatives are real issues missed by the tool.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.120
Duplicate findings may share a single root cause.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:48.060
Environmental issues may reflect test data, staging configuration, or scanner authentication failure rather than product code.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.680
Triage separates signal from noise.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.980
Severity should reflect context.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:00.240
An exposed error on an internal staging-only path may differ from the same behavior on an internet-facing production endpoint.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:06.380
A session issue in an administrative workflow may carry higher impact than one in a low-risk test page.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:15.080
Exposure, data sensitivity, user role, reachable path, business function, and compensating controls all influence prioritization.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.300
Evidence should be useful but controlled.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.840
Reports should not publish credentials, tokens, regulated data, customer records, or destructive request details.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:30.380
Developers need enough information to understand and fix the issue, while the organization still protects sensitive data.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.040
Good DAST reporting balances clarity, safety, and actionability.
